      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Angiotensin II normally acts as a potent positive feedback signal by binding to AT1 receptors on the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex, triggering an enzyme cascade that synthesizes aldosterone. When lisinopril drops angiotensin II levels, this adrenal stimulation is shut off immediately, preventing the kidneys from reabsorbing excess sodium and water. Consequently, higher sodium levels exit the body via urine, which further supports the reduction in intravascular volume and helps sustain the lowered blood pressure.",
