    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok got it. so if the ACE enzyme is being blocked, how does the lisinopril actually interact with the enzyme site? is it a competitive inhibition or something else? i need to be able to explain the binding mechanism specifically.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lisinopril is a reversible, competitive inhibitor that binds directly to the active site of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). Structurally, it mimics the pentapeptide substrate (angiotensin I) by occupying the specific pocket where the peptide's carboxyl group binds, thereby physically blocking the real substrate from undergoing conversion. Because this binding is competitive, the drug increases the apparent Km of the enzyme without altering Vmax, meaning that while the reaction slows down significantly under physiological conditions, higher concentrations of substrate could theoretically overcome the block.",
      "source": "target",
